{#
  Docker daemon configuration.

  live-restore keeps containers running while dockerd restarts, so an
  'apt upgrade' that pulls a new Docker version does not stop them.

  This covers Docker's own containers only. It was added while the kubelet
  reached containerd through cri-dockerd, when every cluster container was also
  a Docker container and a dockerd restart stopped every pod on the node.
  cluster/kubernetes.yml now configures containerd's CRI plugin and the kubelet
  connects to it directly, so nothing the cluster runs passes through dockerd.
  containerd needs no equivalent setting either way — its per-container shim
  processes are not children of the daemon and survive a restart by design.

  Kept because it still protects containers started with docker by hand, and
  because the suspend hook depends on knowing which way this is set: it stops
  Docker's containers explicitly, since with live-restore on, stopping dockerd
  no longer stops them (cluster/templates/k8s-suspend-hook.sh.j2).

  It defers a restart rather than removing it. Containers keep using the
  runtime they were started with, so an upgraded runtime does not apply to
  them, including its security fixes, until they are recreated.

  Docker supports live restore across patch-level daemon upgrades. A larger
  version jump may stop containers regardless, so it is not a guarantee.
#}
{
  "live-restore": {{ docker_live_restore | bool | to_json }}
}
